{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgZszbToYwLGLfwqeMY2w","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018sFDcfjwVkRnkyU7i7TXBR","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/sgd_escape_time_distributions_20261030_140000_math01/paper_draft/sections/abstract.tex","content":"Theories of flat-minima bias and learning-rate scaling rest on mean escape-time formulas, yet the full distribution of how long an optimizer stays in a basin is rarely measured.\nWe measure it.\nWe run SGD and Adam from exact minima and true saddles of small tanh networks on \\fmnist and \\digitsdata, across 165 conditions, 23,104 replicas and 17,474 exits, and decide basin exit by converged quenching.\nOur simulator recovers the exact Kramers rate on a controlled double well (median measured/exact time 1.07).\nWe test four numerical claims, and none holds as stated.\nExponential times are rejected in 86\\% of conditions, but Weibull is the best model in only 4 of 28, its shape $k$ lies in 0.7--1.3 in only 32\\%, and $k$ is unrelated to curvature ($\\rho=-0.06$).\nMost of the deviation from $k=1$ comes from a start-up delay (refitting after early exits gives median $k=1.03$) and from mixing across minima (pooling gives $k=0.52$--$0.74$).\nDoubling the batch size multiplies escape time by 5.1, not $\\sqrt{2}$, while $\\etaB$ still sets the time scale on \\fmnist to within about 10\\%.\nAdam escapes about $2\\times$ faster than SGD at matched in-basin loss, with no demonstrated dependence on basin width, but makes no exits in 150,000 steps at its default learning rate.\nSaddle exit follows the top eigenvector 82--86\\% of the time at low noise and 54\\% at high noise, and the eigenvalue gap does not predict that share.\nEscape is therefore a discrete, delayed hop whose rate depends strongly on noise and on the specific minimum.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4804,"cache_read_input_tokens":115244,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4804},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f205d79e-9dcc-4336-8ac8-c0edce33ebd6","uuid":"bd35cd07-fca7-46e4-82f7-e29457037372","timestamp":"2026-10-31T06:57:52.977Z","request_id":"req_011CgZszb7Tt386YqnLTY1kH","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_018sFDcfjwVkRnkyU7i7TXBR":{"file_path":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/sgd_escape_time_distributions_20261030_140000_math01/paper_draft/sections/abstract.tex","content":"Theories of flat-minima bias and learning-rate scaling rest on mean escape-time formulas, yet the full distribution of how long an optimizer stays in a basin is rarely measured.\nWe measure it.\nWe run SGD and Adam from exact minima and true saddles of small tanh networks on \\fmnist and \\digitsdata, across 165 conditions, 23,104 replicas and 17,474 exits, and decide basin exit by converged quenching.\nOur simulator recovers the exact Kramers rate on a controlled double well (median measured/exact time 1.07).\nWe test four numerical claims, and none holds as stated.\nExponential times are rejected in 86\\% of conditions, but Weibull is the best model in only 4 of 28, its shape $k$ lies in 0.7--1.3 in only 32\\%, and $k$ is unrelated to curvature ($\\rho=-0.06$).\nMost of the deviation from $k=1$ comes from a start-up delay (refitting after early exits gives median $k=1.03$) and from mixing across minima (pooling gives $k=0.52$--$0.74$).\nDoubling the batch size multiplies escape time by 5.1, not $\\sqrt{2}$, while $\\etaB$ still sets the time scale on \\fmnist to within about 10\\%.\nAdam escapes about $2\\times$ faster than SGD at matched in-basin loss, with no demonstrated dependence on basin width, but makes no exits in 150,000 steps at its default learning rate.\nSaddle exit follows the top eigenvector 82--86\\% of the time at low noise and 54\\% at high noise, and the eigenvalue gap does not predict that share.\nEscape is therefore a discrete, delayed hop whose rate depends strongly on noise and on the specific minimum.\n"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018sFDcfjwVkRnkyU7i7TXBR","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/sgd_escape_time_distributions_20261030_140000_math01/paper_draft/sections/abstract.tex (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f205d79e-9dcc-4336-8ac8-c0edce33ebd6","uuid":"1eda2cb6-7b78-427e-b830-3cfc141a2905","timestamp":"2026-10-31T06:57:53.019Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/sgd_escape_time_distributions_20261030_140000_math01/paper_draft/sections/abstract.tex","content":"Theories of flat-minima bias and learning-rate scaling rest on mean escape-time formulas, yet the full distribution of how long an optimizer stays in a basin is rarely measured.\nWe measure it.\nWe run SGD and Adam from exact minima and true saddles of small tanh networks on \\fmnist and \\digitsdata, across 165 conditions, 23,104 replicas and 17,474 exits, and decide basin exit by converged quenching.\nOur simulator recovers the exact Kramers rate on a controlled double well (median measured/exact time 1.07).\nWe test four numerical claims, and none holds as stated.\nExponential times are rejected in 86\\% of conditions, but Weibull is the best model in only 4 of 28, its shape $k$ lies in 0.7--1.3 in only 32\\%, and $k$ is unrelated to curvature ($\\rho=-0.06$).\nMost of the deviation from $k=1$ comes from a start-up delay (refitting after early exits gives median $k=1.03$) and from mixing across minima (pooling gives $k=0.52$--$0.74$).\nDoubling the batch size multiplies escape time by 5.1, not $\\sqrt{2}$, while $\\etaB$ still sets the time scale on \\fmnist to within about 10\\%.\nAdam escapes about $2\\times$ faster than SGD at matched in-basin loss, with no demonstrated dependence on basin width, but makes no exits in 150,000 steps at its default learning rate.\nSaddle exit follows the top eigenvector 82--86\\% of the time at low noise and 54\\% at high noise, and the eigenvalue gap does not predict that share.\nEscape is therefore a discrete, delayed hop whose rate depends strongly on noise and on the specific minimum.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_018sFDcfjwVkRnkyU7i7TXBR","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgZszbToYwLGLfwqeMY2w","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018Kq9AvtHiwK3unN2rMSdzZ","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/sgd_escape_time_distributions_20261030_140000_math01/paper_draft/sections/introduction.tex","content":"\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n\nHow long does an optimizer stay in a loss basin?\nArguments for flat-minima bias, implicit regularization and learning-rate schedules all depend on the answer \\citep{xie2021diffusion,mori2022powerlaw,smith2018bayesian}.\nThose arguments lean on mean escape-time formulas derived in asymptotic limits.\n\nThe mean is not enough.\nThe full distribution tells us whether escape is memoryless, which is what the usual stochastic differential equation (SDE) picture assumes.\nIn the high-barrier limit, Kramers theory predicts Poisson exits and therefore exponential escape times \\citep{kramers1940brownian}.\nReal optimizers run at finite noise, start at a specific point, and move through landscapes with many neighbouring minima.\nNone of these conditions guarantees exponential times, and each one bends the distribution in its own way.\n\nPrior work measures mean rates \\citep{xie2021diffusion,mori2022powerlaw,ibayashi2021exponential}.\nWe found no study of distribution shape with censoring and model comparison in real networks, although keyword searches of arXiv are not proof of absence.\nTheory also disagrees on direction: \\citet{zhou2020towards} predict that SGD escapes a given basin faster than Adam, while a common intuition holds the reverse.\nOn one hand, the SDE view gives clean scaling laws.\nOn the other hand, few measurements test those laws against exact minima and saddles.\n\nWe test four numerical claims about escape.\n(H1) Escape times are Weibull, not exponential, with shape $k$ in 0.7--1.3 depending on geometry.\n(H2) Adam escapes narrow basins 2--5$\\times$ faster than SGD, with similar times at wide basins.\n(H3) Doubling the batch size $B$ at fixed learning rate $\\eta$ multiplies mean escape time by $\\sqrt{2}$, with $B/\\eta$ setting temperature.\n(H4) Hessian eigenvalue gaps at saddles predict the escape direction with 70--85\\% accuracy.\nWe fix the pass criteria before any experiment.\nWe then run hundreds of independent replicas of real SGD and Adam from the same exact minimum or saddle of a small tanh network, and we decide exit by quenching each replica to the minimum it belongs to (see \\algref{alg:quench}).\n\nNone of the four claims holds as stated, and each fails in an informative way.\nExponential times are rejected in 86\\% of conditions, but only 32\\% of fitted $k$ values lie in 0.7--1.3 (median 1.47), and $k$ shows no relation to sharpness.\nDiscarding the earliest quarter of exits moves median $k$ from 1.47 to 1.03.\nDoubling $B$ multiplies escape time by 5.1 [3.4, 8.0], about $\\tau\\propto B^{2}$, not $\\sqrt{B}$.\nAdam is about $2\\times$ faster at matched in-basin loss and slower by at least 7--34$\\times$ at its default learning rate.\nAt low noise, saddle exit follows the top eigenvector in 82--86\\% of runs, yet the gap does not predict that share.\n\n\\noindent Our contributions are:\n\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=0pt,topsep=0pt]\n    \\item We build an escape-time instrument for real networks from exact critical points, converged quenching and interval-censored fits, and we validate it against the exact Kramers rate.\n    \\item We measure the shape of escape-time distributions across 28 well-observed conditions, and we decompose non-exponentiality into a start-up delay and heterogeneity across minima.\n    \\item We show that batch size acts far more strongly than a $\\sqrt{B}$ law, and that the sign of the Adam--SGD comparison depends on learning-rate calibration.\n    \\item We show that saddle exit is highly reproducible even when it leaves the top eigenvector, which a linearized model cannot explain.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\vspace{4pt}\n\\noindent \\Secref{sec:related} reviews prior work, \\secref{sec:method} describes our method, \\secref{sec:results} reports results, and \\secref{sec:discussion} discusses them.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4804,"cache_read_input_tokens":115244,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4804},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f205d79e-9dcc-4336-8ac8-c0edce33ebd6","uuid":"99c77de7-3cc5-430d-9c52-5788cfa8c712","timestamp":"2026-10-31T06:58:06.276Z","request_id":"req_011CgZszb7Tt386YqnLTY1kH","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_018Kq9AvtHiwK3unN2rMSdzZ":{"file_path":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/sgd_escape_time_distributions_20261030_140000_math01/paper_draft/sections/introduction.tex","content":"\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n\nHow long does an optimizer stay in a loss basin?\nArguments for flat-minima bias, implicit regularization and learning-rate schedules all depend on the answer \\citep{xie2021diffusion,mori2022powerlaw,smith2018bayesian}.\nThose arguments lean on mean escape-time formulas derived in asymptotic limits.\n\nThe mean is not enough.\nThe full distribution tells us whether escape is memoryless, which is what the usual stochastic differential equation (SDE) picture assumes.\nIn the high-barrier limit, Kramers theory predicts Poisson exits and therefore exponential escape times \\citep{kramers1940brownian}.\nReal optimizers run at finite noise, start at a specific point, and move through landscapes with many neighbouring minima.\nNone of these conditions guarantees exponential times, and each one bends the distribution in its own way.\n\nPrior work measures mean rates \\citep{xie2021diffusion,mori2022powerlaw,ibayashi2021exponential}.\nWe found no study of distribution shape with censoring and model comparison in real networks, although keyword searches of arXiv are not proof of absence.\nTheory also disagrees on direction: \\citet{zhou2020towards} predict that SGD escapes a given basin faster than Adam, while a common intuition holds the reverse.\nOn one hand, the SDE view gives clean scaling laws.\nOn the other hand, few measurements test those laws against exact minima and saddles.\n\nWe test four numerical claims about escape.\n(H1) Escape times are Weibull, not exponential, with shape $k$ in 0.7--1.3 depending on geometry.\n(H2) Adam escapes narrow basins 2--5$\\times$ faster than SGD, with similar times at wide basins.\n(H3) Doubling the batch size $B$ at fixed learning rate $\\eta$ multiplies mean escape time by $\\sqrt{2}$, with $B/\\eta$ setting temperature.\n(H4) Hessian eigenvalue gaps at saddles predict the escape direction with 70--85\\% accuracy.\nWe fix the pass criteria before any experiment.\nWe then run hundreds of independent replicas of real SGD and Adam from the same exact minimum or saddle of a small tanh network, and we decide exit by quenching each replica to the minimum it belongs to (see \\algref{alg:quench}).\n\nNone of the four claims holds as stated, and each fails in an informative way.\nExponential times are rejected in 86\\% of conditions, but only 32\\% of fitted $k$ values lie in 0.7--1.3 (median 1.47), and $k$ shows no relation to sharpness.\nDiscarding the earliest quarter of exits moves median $k$ from 1.47 to 1.03.\nDoubling $B$ multiplies escape time by 5.1 [3.4, 8.0], about $\\tau\\propto B^{2}$, not $\\sqrt{B}$.\nAdam is about $2\\times$ faster at matched in-basin loss and slower by at least 7--34$\\times$ at its default learning rate.\nAt low noise, saddle exit follows the top eigenvector in 82--86\\% of runs, yet the gap does not predict that share.\n\n\\noindent Our contributions are:\n\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=0pt,topsep=0pt]\n    \\item We build an escape-time instrument for real networks from exact critical points, converged quenching and interval-censored fits, and we validate it against the exact Kramers rate.\n    \\item We measure the shape of escape-time distributions across 28 well-observed conditions, and we decompose non-exponentiality into a start-up delay and heterogeneity across minima.\n    \\item We show that batch size acts far more strongly than a $\\sqrt{B}$ law, and that the sign of the Adam--SGD comparison depends on learning-rate calibration.\n    \\item We show that saddle exit is highly reproducible even when it leaves the top eigenvector, which a linearized model cannot explain.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\vspace{4pt}\n\\noindent \\Secref{sec:related} reviews prior work, \\secref{sec:method} describes our method, \\secref{sec:results} reports results, and \\secref{sec:discussion} discusses them.\n"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018Kq9AvtHiwK3unN2rMSdzZ","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/sgd_escape_time_distributions_20261030_140000_math01/paper_draft/sections/introduction.tex (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f205d79e-9dcc-4336-8ac8-c0edce33ebd6","uuid":"9a7e059c-6333-46f6-945b-fcf505a7a9f7","timestamp":"2026-10-31T06:58:06.316Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/sgd_escape_time_distributions_20261030_140000_math01/paper_draft/sections/introduction.tex","content":"\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n\nHow long does an optimizer stay in a loss basin?\nArguments for flat-minima bias, implicit regularization and learning-rate schedules all depend on the answer \\citep{xie2021diffusion,mori2022powerlaw,smith2018bayesian}.\nThose arguments lean on mean escape-time formulas derived in asymptotic limits.\n\nThe mean is not enough.\nThe full distribution tells us whether escape is memoryless, which is what the usual stochastic differential equation (SDE) picture assumes.\nIn the high-barrier limit, Kramers theory predicts Poisson exits and therefore exponential escape times \\citep{kramers1940brownian}.\nReal optimizers run at finite noise, start at a specific point, and move through landscapes with many neighbouring minima.\nNone of these conditions guarantees exponential times, and each one bends the distribution in its own way.\n\nPrior work measures mean rates \\citep{xie2021diffusion,mori2022powerlaw,ibayashi2021exponential}.\nWe found no study of distribution shape with censoring and model comparison in real networks, although keyword searches of arXiv are not proof of absence.\nTheory also disagrees on direction: \\citet{zhou2020towards} predict that SGD escapes a given basin faster than Adam, while a common intuition holds the reverse.\nOn one hand, the SDE view gives clean scaling laws.\nOn the other hand, few measurements test those laws against exact minima and saddles.\n\nWe test four numerical claims about escape.\n(H1) Escape times are Weibull, not exponential, with shape $k$ in 0.7--1.3 depending on geometry.\n(H2) Adam escapes narrow basins 2--5$\\times$ faster than SGD, with similar times at wide basins.\n(H3) Doubling the batch size $B$ at fixed learning rate $\\eta$ multiplies mean escape time by $\\sqrt{2}$, with $B/\\eta$ setting temperature.\n(H4) Hessian eigenvalue gaps at saddles predict the escape direction with 70--85\\% accuracy.\nWe fix the pass criteria before any experiment.\nWe then run hundreds of independent replicas of real SGD and Adam from the same exact minimum or saddle of a small tanh network, and we decide exit by quenching each replica to the minimum it belongs to (see \\algref{alg:quench}).\n\nNone of the four claims holds as stated, and each fails in an informative way.\nExponential times are rejected in 86\\% of conditions, but only 32\\% of fitted $k$ values lie in 0.7--1.3 (median 1.47), and $k$ shows no relation to sharpness.\nDiscarding the earliest quarter of exits moves median $k$ from 1.47 to 1.03.\nDoubling $B$ multiplies escape time by 5.1 [3.4, 8.0], about $\\tau\\propto B^{2}$, not $\\sqrt{B}$.\nAdam is about $2\\times$ faster at matched in-basin loss and slower by at least 7--34$\\times$ at its default learning rate.\nAt low noise, saddle exit follows the top eigenvector in 82--86\\% of runs, yet the gap does not predict that share.\n\n\\noindent Our contributions are:\n\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=0pt,topsep=0pt]\n    \\item We build an escape-time instrument for real networks from exact critical points, converged quenching and interval-censored fits, and we validate it against the exact Kramers rate.\n    \\item We measure the shape of escape-time distributions across 28 well-observed conditions, and we decompose non-exponentiality into a start-up delay and heterogeneity across minima.\n    \\item We show that batch size acts far more strongly than a $\\sqrt{B}$ law, and that the sign of the Adam--SGD comparison depends on learning-rate calibration.\n    \\item We show that saddle exit is highly reproducible even when it leaves the top eigenvector, which a linearized model cannot explain.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\vspace{4pt}\n\\noindent \\Secref{sec:related} reviews prior work, \\secref{sec:method} describes our method, \\secref{sec:results} reports results, and \\secref{sec:discussion} discusses them.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_018Kq9AvtHiwK3unN2rMSdzZ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgZszbToYwLGLfwqeMY2w","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01G7yQ3Wrh1XhME5uaZYQnAT","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/sgd_escape_time_distributions_20261030_140000_math01/paper_draft/sections/related_work.tex","content":"\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n\n\\para{Kramers escape.}\n\\citet{kramers1940brownian} gives the overdamped rate over a barrier $\\Delta E$ at temperature $T$ as $\\kappa=\\frac{\\sqrt{a|b|}}{2\\pi}\\exp(-\\Delta E/T)$, with $a$ and $b$ the curvatures at the well and the saddle.\nAt high barrier the exit is a Poisson process, so first-passage times are exponential ($k=1$).\nAny deviation must come from outside that limit, such as a relaxation transient, a low barrier or a mixture of rates.\nWe use this exact result to validate our instrument, and then ask which of these sources act in real networks.\n\n\\para{SGD as an SDE.}\n\\citet{mandt2017stochastic}, \\citet{smith2018bayesian} and \\citet{jastrzebski2018three} derive a temperature that scales as $\\etaB$.\n\\citet{zhu2019anisotropic} show that anisotropic noise aligned with sharp directions speeds escape from sharp minima.\nWith noise covariance close to the Hessian, \\citet{xie2021diffusion} find mean escape time exponential in $(B/\\eta)\\,\\Delta L/H$.\nWith noise proportional to the loss, \\citet{mori2022powerlaw} find a rate that is a power law in the ratio $L(\\vtheta_s)/L(\\tstar)$, while $\\log\\tau$ stays linear in $B/\\eta$.\n\\citet{ibayashi2021exponential} prove exponential escape efficiency without a stationary distribution, and \\citet{simsekli2019tail} argue for heavy-tailed noise, whose L\\'evy models give a polynomial dependence on barrier height.\nNo theory above predicts $\\tau\\propto\\sqrt{B}$.\nUnlike this line of work, which studies mean rates, we measure the distribution and its censored tail.\n\n\\para{Adam versus SGD.}\n\\citet{zhou2020towards} show with a L\\'evy SDE that, for the same basin, SGD has a smaller escape time than Adam (\\citet{kingma2015adam}), because adaptive scaling enlarges the basin's Radon measure and smooths noise tails.\n\\citet{xie2022adaptive} separate the two parts of Adam: adaptive learning rates escape saddles efficiently but do not select flat minima as SGD does, while momentum helps pass saddles and hardly affects flat-minima selection.\nWe found no direct measurement of an Adam-to-SGD escape ratio split by basin width.\nAny such ratio depends on how the two learning rates are matched, so we make that calibration explicit and report how the result changes with it.\n\n\\para{Saddles.}\n\\citet{jin2017escape} and \\citet{daneshmand2018escaping} bound the rate of saddle escape under perturbation or intrinsic gradient noise, and neither claims which negative eigendirection wins.\n\\citet{fukumizu2000local} and \\citet{simsek2021geometry} show that unit duplication and symmetry create true saddles in small networks.\nWe add a measurement of the winning direction at nonsymmetric saddles found by mode-following Newton, and we compare it with a linearized model that uses the measured noise covariance.\n\n\\para{Sharpness caveats.}\n\\citet{keskar2017large} link sharpness to large-batch training, and \\citet{dinh2017sharp} show that sharpness is not invariant to reparameterization.\nWe report $\\lmax$ and trace in one fixed parameterization and use them descriptively.\n\\citet{yaida2019fluctuation} give fluctuation-dissipation checks for stationarity, which motivates our attention to the transient that follows a start at $\\tstar$.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4804,"cache_read_input_tokens":115244,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4804},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f205d79e-9dcc-4336-8ac8-c0edce33ebd6","uuid":"c545c4c0-d86d-41c6-8bdf-db1af1de38af","timestamp":"2026-10-31T06:58:18.076Z","request_id":"req_011CgZszb7Tt386YqnLTY1kH","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01G7yQ3Wrh1XhME5uaZYQnAT":{"file_path":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/sgd_escape_time_distributions_20261030_140000_math01/paper_draft/sections/related_work.tex","content":"\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n\n\\para{Kramers escape.}\n\\citet{kramers1940brownian} gives the overdamped rate over a barrier $\\Delta E$ at temperature $T$ as $\\kappa=\\frac{\\sqrt{a|b|}}{2\\pi}\\exp(-\\Delta E/T)$, with $a$ and $b$ the curvatures at the well and the saddle.\nAt high barrier the exit is a Poisson process, so first-passage times are exponential ($k=1$).\nAny deviation must come from outside that limit, such as a relaxation transient, a low barrier or a mixture of rates.\nWe use this exact result to validate our instrument, and then ask which of these sources act in real networks.\n\n\\para{SGD as an SDE.}\n\\citet{mandt2017stochastic}, \\citet{smith2018bayesian} and \\citet{jastrzebski2018three} derive a temperature that scales as $\\etaB$.\n\\citet{zhu2019anisotropic} show that anisotropic noise aligned with sharp directions speeds escape from sharp minima.\nWith noise covariance close to the Hessian, \\citet{xie2021diffusion} find mean escape time exponential in $(B/\\eta)\\,\\Delta L/H$.\nWith noise proportional to the loss, \\citet{mori2022powerlaw} find a rate that is a power law in the ratio $L(\\vtheta_s)/L(\\tstar)$, while $\\log\\tau$ stays linear in $B/\\eta$.\n\\citet{ibayashi2021exponential} prove exponential escape efficiency without a stationary distribution, and \\citet{simsekli2019tail} argue for heavy-tailed noise, whose L\\'evy models give a polynomial dependence on barrier height.\nNo theory above predicts $\\tau\\propto\\sqrt{B}$.\nUnlike this line of work, which studies mean rates, we measure the distribution and its censored tail.\n\n\\para{Adam versus SGD.}\n\\citet{zhou2020towards} show with a L\\'evy SDE that, for the same basin, SGD has a smaller escape time than Adam (\\citet{kingma2015adam}), because adaptive scaling enlarges the basin's Radon measure and smooths noise tails.\n\\citet{xie2022adaptive} separate the two parts of Adam: adaptive learning rates escape saddles efficiently but do not select flat minima as SGD does, while momentum helps pass saddles and hardly affects flat-minima selection.\nWe found no direct measurement of an Adam-to-SGD escape ratio split by basin width.\nAny such ratio depends on how the two learning rates are matched, so we make that calibration explicit and report how the result changes with it.\n\n\\para{Saddles.}\n\\citet{jin2017escape} and \\citet{daneshmand2018escaping} bound the rate of saddle escape under perturbation or intrinsic gradient noise, and neither claims which negative eigendirection wins.\n\\citet{fukumizu2000local} and \\citet{simsek2021geometry} show that unit duplication and symmetry create true saddles in small networks.\nWe add a measurement of the winning direction at nonsymmetric saddles found by mode-following Newton, and we compare it with a linearized model that uses the measured noise covariance.\n\n\\para{Sharpness caveats.}\n\\citet{keskar2017large} link sharpness to large-batch training, and \\citet{dinh2017sharp} show that sharpness is not invariant to reparameterization.\nWe report $\\lmax$ and trace in one fixed parameterization and use them descriptively.\n\\citet{yaida2019fluctuation} give fluctuation-dissipation checks for stationarity, which motivates our attention to the transient that follows a start at $\\tstar$.\n"}}}
