iency without a stationary distribution, and \\citet{simsekli2019tail} argue for heavy-tailed noise, whose L\\'evy models give a polynomial dependence on barrier height.\nNo theory above predicts $\\tau\\propto\\sqrt{B}$.\nUnlike this line of work, which studies mean rates, we measure the distribution and its censored tail.\n\n\\para{Adam versus SGD.}\n\\citet{zhou2020towards} show with a L\\'evy SDE that, for the same basin, SGD has a smaller escape time than Adam (\\citet{kingma2015adam}), because adaptive scaling enlarges the basin's Radon measure and smooths noise tails.\n\\citet{xie2022adaptive} separate the two parts of Adam: adaptive learning rates escape saddles efficiently but do not select flat minima as SGD does, while momentum helps pass saddles and hardly affects flat-minima selection.\nWe found no direct measurement of an Adam-to-SGD escape ratio split by basin width.\nAny such ratio depends on how the two learning rates are matched, so we make that calibration explicit and report how the result changes with it.\n\n\\para{Saddles.}\n\\citet{jin2017escape} and \\citet{daneshmand2018escaping} bound the rate of saddle escape under perturbation or intrinsic gradient noise, and neither claims which negative eigendirection wins.\n\\citet{fukumizu2000local} and \\citet{simsek2021geometry} show that unit duplication and symmetry create true saddles in small networks.\nWe add a measurement of the winning direction at nonsymmetric saddles found by mode-following Newton, and we compare it with a linearized model that uses the measured noise covariance.\n\n\\para{Sharpness caveats.}\n\\citet{keskar2017large} link sharpness to large-batch training, and \\citet{dinh2017sharp} show that sharpness is not invariant to reparameterization.\nWe report $\\lmax$ and trace in one fixed parameterization and use them descriptively.\n\\citet{yaida2019fluctuation} give fluctuation-dissipation checks for stationarity, which motivates our attention to the transient that follows a start at $\\tstar$.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01G7yQ3Wrh1XhME5uaZYQnAT","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgZszbToYwLGLfwqeMY2w","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LghdZW9Wm7zTwGu8fGNths","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/sgd_escape_time_distributions_20261030_140000_math01/paper_draft/sections/methodology.tex","content":"\\section{Method}\n\\label{sec:method}\n\n\\para{Problem formulation.}\nLet $\\tstar$ be a strict local minimum of a regularized loss $L$, with Hessian eigenvalues up to $\\lmax$.\nWe start $R$ independent replicas of an optimizer at $\\tstar$, with separate minibatch draws.\nThe escape time $T$ of a replica is the first step at which gradient flow from $\\vtheta_t$ no longer returns to $\\tstar$.\nWe write $\\tau$ for the Kaplan--Meier median of $T$.\nWe compare the Weibull survival function with its rivals,\n\\begin{equation}\n    S(t)=\\exp\\big(-(t/\\lambda)^{k}\\big),\n    \\label{eq:weibull}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $k=1$ is the exponential law of Kramers escape, $k>1$ means a rising hazard, and $k<1$ means a falling hazard.\n\n\\para{Models and data.}\nWe choose one-hidden-layer tanh networks with cross entropy plus L2, because tanh gives exact Hessians, and because L2 and underparameterization give strict minima with nonzero per-sample gradients.\n\\Tabref{tab:landscapes} lists the three landscapes.\nWe take 12 minima on landscape A and 6 on landscape B, chosen evenly in $\\lmax$ rank before any escape measurement and Newton-polished in float64 ($\\|\\nabla L\\|<10^{-7}$, positive definite Hessian).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\caption{The three landscapes. All networks have one tanh hidden layer of width $h$. Inputs for A and S are 24 PCA dimensions.}\n    \\label{tab:landscapes}\n    \\begin{tabular}{@{}lllcccl@{}}\n        \\toprule\n        & \\textbf{Role} & \\textbf{Data} & $h$ & \\textbf{Weight decay} & \\textbf{Parameters} & $\\lmax$ \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        A & Main & \\fmnist \\citep{xiao2017fashion}, 2,000 examples & 8 & $10^{-4}$ & 290 & 0.62--5.48 \\\\\n        B & Replication & \\digitsdata \\citep{alpaydin1998optical}, 1,797 examples & 8 & $10^{-3}$ & 610 & 0.34--0.58 \\\\\n        S & Saddles & \\fmnist, as A & 3 & $10^{-4}$ & 115 & -- \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\para{Optimizers.}\nWe run SGD and Adam ($\\beta$ values 0.9 and 0.999), with ablations for \\rmsprop, \\sgdm ($\\beta=0.9$, $\\eta=0.01$, same effective step as SGD at $\\eta=0.1$), and warm-started Adam.\nEach replica draws its own fixed-size minibatches.\nWe use $R=256$ at the base condition and 128 elsewhere (64 for default Adam), with a cap of 150,000 steps.\nThe simulation runs in float32 on shared GPUs (about 29 worker-hours); fits and Hessians use the CPU.\n\n\\para{{\\bf What counts as an exit?}}\nAt geometrically spaced checks (10\\% spacing, minimum gap 100 steps), we copy each replica and relax the copy by full-batch heavy-ball descent (\\algref{alg:quench}).\nA replica has exited if the relaxed point converges to a different minimum.\nTimes are interval-censored between checks.\n\n\\begin{algorithm}[t]\n\\caption{Exit check for one replica}\n\\label{alg:quench}\n\\begin{algorithmic}[1]\n\\State \\textbf{Input:} replica $\\vtheta_t$, minimum $\\tstar$, loss $L$, sharpness $\\lmax$\n\\State $q \\gets \\max(1,\\lmax)$; \\quad $\\tilde\\vtheta \\gets \\vtheta_t$ \\Comment{\\rightcomment{work on a copy}}\n\\State Relax $\\tilde\\vtheta$ for $1{,}500\\,q$ heavy-ball steps ($\\text{lr}=1/\\lmax$, $\\beta=0.9$)\n\\If{$\\|\\tilde\\vtheta-\\tstar\\|\\le 0.3$} \\textbf{return} in basin \\Comment{\\rightcomment{cheap gate}}\n\\EndIf\n\\State Continue relaxing up to $5{,}000\\,q$ total steps\n\\State $d\\gets\\|\\tilde\\vtheta-\\tstar\\|$; \\quad $b\\gets$ barrier on the segment from $\\tilde\\vtheta$ to $\\tstar$; \\quad $\\Delta L\\gets L(\\tilde\\vtheta)-L(\\tstar)$\n\\If{$d>0.05$ \\textbf{and} ($b>10^{-5}$ \\textbf{or} $|\\Delta L|>2\\times10^{-5}$)} \\textbf{return} exited\n\\EndIf\n\\State \\textbf{return} in basin \\Comment{\\rightcomment{slow soft mode, not a new minimum}}\n\\end{algorithmic}\n\\end{algorithm}\n\nThis rule survived two failures, both found by testing it.\nFirst, short quenches mislead: a 400-step quench reported 0--34\\% exited where a converged quench reported up to 97\\%.\nSecond, slow soft modes give false exits: a Newton audit found that 495 of the first 6,337 exits (7.8\\%) polished back to $\\tstar$, and in one minimum 71\\% of exits were false.\nWe added the barrier-or-loss rule and repeated the affected runs with the same seeds.\nA barrier test alone would be wrong, because one \\digitsdata minimum has a real neighbour with no segment barrier.\n\n\\para{Experiments.}\nE0 is a controlled 8-dimensional double well in the Gaussian minibatch-noise limit, with 1,000 replicas per condition.\nIt validates the instrument against Kramers, and it does not model a network.\nE1 measures shape at all minima with SGD, $B=16$, $\\eta\\in\\{0.1,0.2\\}$.\nE2 compares Adam with SGD at Adam learning rates $\\{0.004,0.008,0.016\\}$ on A and $\\{0.001,0.002\\}$ on B.\nWe interpolate the median in $\\log\\eta$ to the per-minimum matched rate, where matched means equal mean excess full loss over steps 500--2,000, well before most exits.\nMatched rates are 0.0055--0.0109 on A and 0.00135--0.00152 on B.\nWe define wide and narrow as the bottom and top thirds by $\\lmax$ ($n=4$ each on A).\nE3 uses 6 (A) and 3 (B) minima with $B\\in\\{4,8,16,32,64\\}$ at $\\eta=0.1$, plus the equal-ratio triple $(\\eta,B)\\in\\{(0.05,8),(0.1,16),(0.2,32)\\}$.\nE4 uses 30 unique saddles from mode-following Newton ($\\|\\nabla L\\|<10^{-9}$), mostly index 2, with $R=128$ and $(\\eta,B)\\in\\{(0.1,16),(0.02,16),(0.1,64)\\}$.\nThe winning eigenvector has the largest absolute projection at first crossing of radius $r$ in the negative subspace.\n\n\\para{Baselines.}\nFor shape, the baseline is the exponential law, tested against Weibull, gamma, lognormal and shifted exponential.\nFor H3, the rival to a power law in $B$ is the Arrhenius form with $\\log\\tau$ linear in $B$.\nFor saddles, the baselines are chance (0.46 on average) and a linearized Monte-Carlo model with the measured per-sample noise covariance $\\sigma_i$, in which component $i$ grows as\n\\begin{equation}\n    z_i\\,\\sigma_i\\,(1+\\eta|\\lambda_i|)^{t},\\qquad z_i\\sim\\gN(0,1).\n    \\label{eq:linear}\n\\end{equation}\n\n\\para{Statistics.}\nWe fit all models by interval-censored maximum likelihood.\nOn simulated data, $k_{\\text{true}}=0.7,1.0,1.3$ gives 0.71, 1.03, 1.29, with 5\\% false rejection of $k=1$.\nWe apply Benjamini--Hochberg control at 5\\% to likelihood-ratio tests.\nConfidence intervals for $k$ use 100 bootstrap replicates of replicas, and cross-minimum intervals bootstrap {\\em minima}, the unit of generalization.\nWhen the median is not reached, we hold $\\tau$ at the 150,000-step lower bound and flag it; Weibull extrapolations reached $10^{9}$.\nFlagged medians are excluded from slope and factor fits.\nH1 analyses use conditions with at least 50\\% exits (28 of 36 E1 conditions).\nAll intervals are 95\\%.\n\\Tabref{tab:prereg} in \\appref{app:prereg} lists the criteria we fixed in advance.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4804,"cache_read_input_tokens":115244,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4804},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f205d79e-9dcc-4336-8ac8-c0edce33ebd6","uuid":"881ad1fe-e0fb-4d8b-abbb-2829165e3c9e","timestamp":"2026-10-31T06:58:43.030Z","request_id":"req_011CgZszb7Tt386YqnLTY1kH","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01LghdZW9Wm7zTwGu8fGNths":{"file_path":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/sgd_escape_time_distributions_20261030_140000_math01/paper_draft/sections/methodology.tex","content":"\\section{Method}\n\\label{sec:method}\n\n\\para{Problem formulation.}\nLet $\\tstar$ be a strict local minimum of a regularized loss $L$, with Hessian eigenvalues up to $\\lmax$.\nWe start $R$ independent replicas of an optimizer at $\\tstar$, with separate minibatch draws.\nThe escape time $T$ of a replica is the first step at which gradient flow from $\\vtheta_t$ no longer returns to $\\tstar$.\nWe write $\\tau$ for the Kaplan--Meier median of $T$.\nWe compare the Weibull survival function with its rivals,\n\\begin{equation}\n    S(t)=\\exp\\big(-(t/\\lambda)^{k}\\big),\n    \\label{eq:weibull}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $k=1$ is the exponential law of Kramers escape, $k>1$ means a rising hazard, and $k<1$ means a falling hazard.\n\n\\para{Models and data.}\nWe choose one-hidden-layer tanh networks with cross entropy plus L2, because tanh gives exact Hessians, and because L2 and underparameterization give strict minima with nonzero per-sample gradients.\n\\Tabref{tab:landscapes} lists the three landscapes.\nWe take 12 minima on landscape A and 6 on landscape B, chosen evenly in $\\lmax$ rank before any escape measurement and Newton-polished in float64 ($\\|\\nabla L\\|<10^{-7}$, positive definite Hessian).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\caption{The three landscapes. All networks have one tanh hidden layer of width $h$. Inputs for A and S are 24 PCA dimensions.}\n    \\label{tab:landscapes}\n    \\begin{tabular}{@{}lllcccl@{}}\n        \\toprule\n        & \\textbf{Role} & \\textbf{Data} & $h$ & \\textbf{Weight decay} & \\textbf{Parameters} & $\\lmax$ \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        A & Main & \\fmnist \\citep{xiao2017fashion}, 2,000 examples & 8 & $10^{-4}$ & 290 & 0.62--5.48 \\\\\n        B & Replication & \\digitsdata \\citep{alpaydin1998optical}, 1,797 examples & 8 & $10^{-3}$ & 610 & 0.34--0.58 \\\\\n        S & Saddles & \\fmnist, as A & 3 & $10^{-4}$ & 115 & -- \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\para{Optimizers.}\nWe run SGD and Adam ($\\beta$ values 0.9 and 0.999), with ablations for \\rmsprop, \\sgdm ($\\beta=0.9$, $\\eta=0.01$, same effective step as SGD at $\\eta=0.1$), and warm-started Adam.\nEach replica draws its own fixed-size minibatches.\nWe use $R=256$ at the base condition and 128 elsewhere (64 for default Adam), with a cap of 150,000 steps.\nThe simulation runs in float32 on shared GPUs (about 29 worker-hours); fits and Hessians use the CPU.\n\n\\para{{\\bf What counts as an exit?}}\nAt geometrically spaced checks (10\\% spacing, minimum gap 100 steps), we copy each replica and relax the copy by full-batch heavy-ball descent (\\algref{alg:quench}).\nA replica has exited if the relaxed point converges to a different minimum.\nTimes are interval-censored between checks.\n\n\\begin{algorithm}[t]\n\\caption{Exit check for one replica}\n\\label{alg:quench}\n\\begin{algorithmic}[1]\n\\State \\textbf{Input:} replica $\\vtheta_t$, minimum $\\tstar$, loss $L$, sharpness $\\lmax$\n\\State $q \\gets \\max(1,\\lmax)$; \\quad $\\tilde\\vtheta \\gets \\vtheta_t$ \\Comment{\\rightcomment{work on a copy}}\n\\State Relax $\\tilde\\vtheta$ for $1{,}500\\,q$ heavy-ball steps ($\\text{lr}=1/\\lmax$, $\\beta=0.9$)\n\\If{$\\|\\tilde\\vtheta-\\tstar\\|\\le 0.3$} \\textbf{return} in basin \\Comment{\\rightcomment{cheap gate}}\n\\EndIf\n\\State Continue relaxing up to $5{,}000\\,q$ total steps\n\\State $d\\gets\\|\\tilde\\vtheta-\\tstar\\|$; \\quad $b\\gets$ barrier on the segment from $\\tilde\\vtheta$ to $\\tstar$; \\quad $\\Delta L\\gets L(\\tilde\\vtheta)-L(\\tstar)$\n\\If{$d>0.05$ \\textbf{and} ($b>10^{-5}$ \\textbf{or} $|\\Delta L|>2\\times10^{-5}$)} \\textbf{return} exited\n\\EndIf\n\\State \\textbf{return} in basin \\Comment{\\rightcomment{slow soft mode, not a new minimum}}\n\\end{algorithmic}\n\\end{algorithm}\n\nThis rule survived two failures, both found by testing it.\nFirst, short quenches mislead: a 400-step quench reported 0--34\\% exited where a converged quench reported up to 97\\%.\nSecond, slow soft modes give false exits: a Newton audit found that 495 of the first 6,337 exits (7.8\\%) polished back to $\\tstar$, and in one minimum 71\\% of exits were false.\nWe added the barrier-or-loss rule and repeated the affected runs with the same seeds.\nA barrier test alone would be wrong, because one \\digitsdata minimum has a real neighbour with no segment barrier.\n\n\\para{Experiments.}\nE0 is a controlled 8-dimensional double well in the Gaussian minibatch-noise limit, with 1,000 replicas per condition.\nIt validates the instrument against Kramers, and it does not model a network.\nE1 measures shape at all minima with SGD, $B=16$, $\\eta\\in\\{0.1,0.2\\}$.\nE2 compares Adam with SGD at Adam learning rates $\\{0.004,0.008,0.016\\}$ on A and $\\{0.001,0.002\\}$ on B.\nWe interpolate the median in $\\log\\eta$ to the per-minimum matched rate, where matched means equal mean excess full loss over steps 500--2,000, well before most exits.\nMatched rates are 0.0055--0.0109 on A and 0.00135--0.00152 on B.\nWe define wide and narrow as the bottom and top thirds by $\\lmax$ ($n=4$ each on A).\nE3 uses 6 (A) and 3 (B) minima with $B\\in\\{4,8,16,32,64\\}$ at $\\eta=0.1$, plus the equal-ratio triple $(\\eta,B)\\in\\{(0.05,8),(0.1,16),(0.2,32)\\}$.\nE4 uses 30 unique saddles from mode-following Newton ($\\|\\nabla L\\|<10^{-9}$), mostly index 2, with $R=128$ and $(\\eta,B)\\in\\{(0.1,16),(0.02,16),(0.1,64)\\}$.\nThe winning eigenvector has the largest absolute projection at first crossing of radius $r$ in the negative subspace.\n\n\\para{Baselines.}\nFor shape, the baseline is the exponential law, tested against Weibull, gamma, lognormal and shifted exponential.\nFor H3, the rival to a power law in $B$ is the Arrhenius form with $\\log\\tau$ linear in $B$.\nFor saddles, the baselines are chance (0.46 on average) and a linearized Monte-Carlo model with the measured per-sample noise covariance $\\sigma_i$, in which component $i$ grows as\n\\begin{equation}\n    z_i\\,\\sigma_i\\,(1+\\eta|\\lambda_i|)^{t},\\qquad z_i\\sim\\gN(0,1).\n    \\label{eq:linear}\n\\end{equation}\n\n\\para{Statistics.}\nWe fit all models by interval-censored maximum likelihood.\nOn simulated data, $k_{\\text{true}}=0.7,1.0,1.3$ gives 0.71, 1.03, 1.29, with 5\\% false rejection of $k=1$.\nWe apply Benjamini--Hochberg control at 5\\% to likelihood-ratio tests.\nConfidence intervals for $k$ use 100 bootstrap replicates of replicas, and cross-minimum intervals bootstrap {\\em minima}, the unit of generalization.\nWhen the median is not reached, we hold $\\tau$ at the 150,000-step lower bound and flag it; Weibull extrapolations reached $10^{9}$.\nFlagged medians are excluded from slope and factor fits.\nH1 analyses use conditions with at least 50\\% exits (28 of 36 E1 conditions).\nAll intervals are 95\\%.\n\\Tabref{tab:prereg} in \\appref{app:prereg} lists the criteria we fixed in advance.\n"}}}
