 is outside the 28 conditions in \\tabref{tab:shape}. \\figright Weibull $k$ against $\\lmax$. Most values lie above the hypothesized band, and no trend with $\\lmax$ appears.}\n    \\label{fig:distributions}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\para{Why $k\\ne1$.}\nTwo mechanisms account for most of the deviation.\n{\\em Delay:} discarding the first 25\\% of exits and refitting residual time gives median $k=1.03$ (IQR 0.90--1.20), and 50\\% of conditions fall within 0.85--1.15, against 18\\% before truncation.\nDiscarding 50\\% gives 1.05.\n{\\em Heterogeneity:} pooling minima gives $k=0.74$ and 0.59 on \\fmnist and 0.52 and 0.57 on \\digitsdata, against within-minimum means of 1.76, 1.51, 2.32 and 1.61.\nPooling alone flips the sign of $k-1$.\nEscape is still not fully memoryless after the delay.\nHazards rise and then fall late (\\figref{fig:hazard}), and residual $k$ still ranges from 0.74 to 1.79, so a constant hazard after a delay is only an approximation.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=0.55\\linewidth]{figures/fig3_hazard.png}\n    \\caption{Exit hazard at three minima. The hazard rises during the early delay and falls late, including at minima with very different $k$. A Weibull with $k>1$ cannot show the late fall.}\n    \\label{fig:hazard}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{H2: Adam against SGD}\n\\label{sec:h2}\n\n{\\bf Is Adam faster at narrow basins only?}\nNo.\nAt matched in-basin loss, Adam is about twice as fast across the board, with no demonstrated dependence on width (\\tabref{tab:adam}, \\figref{fig:adam}).\nThe narrow point estimate of 2.75 is in the hypothesized range, but its interval [1.89, 5.18] is wider than [2, 5], so H2a fails its criterion.\nH2b fails because the wide ratio is 1.89 and its interval excludes 1.\nH2c is not significant: the narrow/wide ratio is 1.46 [0.74, 3.09], and the Spearman correlation of ratio with $\\log\\lmax$ is 0.47 ($p=.12$).\nWith $n=12$ we cannot exclude a moderate effect.\nKeeping only minima with observed medians brings narrow and wide nearly level, at 2.01 and 1.91.\nOn \\digitsdata the ratio is 1.56 [0.90, 3.22], with 5 of 6 minima involving a censored median, so this is weak evidence.\n\n\\input{tables/adam}\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=0.55\\linewidth]{figures/fig4_adam_sgd.png}\n    \\caption{Adam speed-up against $\\lmax$ on \\fmnist, at matched in-basin loss. Every minimum has ratio above 1, and the spread shows no clear trend with sharpness. Open markers involve a censored median and are bounds.}\n    \\label{fig:adam}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\para{Ablations.}\n\\rmsprop reproduces Adam's speed-up without momentum, while \\sgdm at the same effective $\\eta$ lands on the same median check as SGD at all 6 minima (resolution about 10\\%).\nSo the adaptive scaling is the source, and warm-starting Adam shows that it is not a cold-start artefact.\nCalibration can reverse the direction: at its default learning rate of 0.001, Adam made no exits in 150,000 steps at 5 of 5 tested minima.\nThat is at least 7--34$\\times$ slower than SGD at the 4 minima where SGD's median is observed.\n\nIn the double well with theory-matched Adam, $\\tau_{\\text{SGD}}/\\tau_{\\text{Adam}}$ is 0.68--0.80 in narrow wells and 0.87--1.27 in wide wells.\nSo Adam shows no consistent speed-up on a single barrier, even with curvature-aligned noise.\nThe network speed-up is therefore not a generic property of adaptive scaling, and we did not test its cause.\n\n\\subsection{H3: batch size and temperature}\n\\label{sec:h3}\n\n{\\bf Does doubling $B$ multiply $\\tau$ by $\\sqrt{2}$?}\nNo.\nThe factor is 5.1 [3.4, 8.0] on \\fmnist, and the slope is 2.02 [1.64, 2.41] against the predicted 0.5, with per-minimum slopes of 1.5--2.8 (\\tabref{tab:batch}, \\figref{fig:batch}).\nThe structural part of H3 does hold: within the observed range, a power law in $B$ fits better than $\\log\\tau$ linear in $B$.\nThis comparison needs care.\nIt uses uncensored medians only, and Arrhenius predicts its fastest growth at large $B$, exactly where censoring removes data.\nThe falling step factors could also reflect only fast minima surviving to large $B$.\nSo our data favour a power law within the observed range, which is mostly $B=4$--32, and they do not rule out Arrhenius behaviour in general.\nIn E0, a single barrier, $\\log\\tau$ stays linear in $1/T$ even with loss-proportional noise, so that noise alone does not explain the network law.\n\n\\input{tables/batch}\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=0.95\\linewidth]{figures/fig5_batch_scaling.png}\n    \\caption{Median escape time against batch size at $\\eta=0.1$. Each gray line is one minimum. Measured growth far exceeds both $\\tau\\propto\\sqrt{B}$ and $\\tau\\propto B$. Open markers at the dotted line are minima whose median exceeds the 150,000-step cap.}\n    \\label{fig:batch}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\para{Equal $\\etaB$.}\n{\\bf Is SDE time $\\tau\\eta$ invariant at fixed $\\etaB$?}\nOn \\fmnist, approximately.\nAcross the 5 minima with observed medians, $\\tau\\eta$ at $(0.05,8)$ over $(0.1,16)$ is 1.03 [0.97, 1.11], and at $(0.2,32)$ over $(0.1,16)$ it is 0.89 [0.86, 0.93], while raw steps differ by $2\\times$ per pair.\nThe second interval excludes 1, but the gap is about one check spacing, so SDE time is the right clock to within about 10\\%.\nIncluding the sixth minimum, capped at all three settings, gives 0.94 [0.72, 1.10] and 1.05 [0.87, 1.38]; these wider intervals partly reflect the ratios of the cap itself.\n\nOn \\digitsdata the test is close to uninformative, and we report it as inconclusive.\nAll 3 medians at $(0.05,8)$ are capped, so the reported ratio of 0.55 [0.50, 0.67] mostly reflects the cap.\nThe ratio at $(0.2,32)$ over $(0.1,16)$ is 1.28 [1.02, 2.00], but only one minimum has both medians observed, and its ratio is 1.03.\n\nDoubling $\\eta$ at fixed $B$ cuts $\\tau$ by 9.4$\\times$ [7.0, 13.4] on \\fmnist.\nTwo of the 12 minima enter this figure at the cap; the other 10 give a geometric mean of 8.5$\\times$.\nThis agrees with $2\\times$ from time rescaling times the $B$ factor above (4.2$\\times$ at $B=16\\to32$).\n\n\\subsection{H4: saddle exit direction}\n\\label{sec:h4}\n\n{\\bf Does the top eigenvector win?}\nIt depends on noise (\\tabref{tab:saddles}, \\figref{fig:saddles}).\nAt lower noise and $r=1$, the share along $\\etop$ is 82--86\\%, which overlaps the 70--85\\% range.\nAt high noise the share is 54\\%, and its interval includes chance.\nWe did not fix the radius in advance, so we report all three.\nAt $r=1$ and low noise we dropped 3 of 30 saddles for too few crossings, which may flatter that cell.\n\n{\\bf Does the gap predict accuracy?}\nNo.\nSpearman $\\rho$ of the $\\etop$ share with relative gap is $-0.07$ (high noise), $+0.03$ (medium) and $+0.23$ (low), all non-significant ($p\\ge.25$).\nFor absolute gap, $\\rho$ runs from $-0.24$ to $-0.07$, also non-significant.\nMost saddles here have a large relative gap, so range restriction limits what a gap test can see.\n\n\\input{tables/saddles}\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=0.95\\linewidth]{figures/fig6_saddles.png}\n    \\caption{Saddle exit direction. \\figleft Share along $\\etop$ against exit radius at three noise levels. The gray band is the hypothesized 70--85\\%. \\figright At high noise and $r=1$, measured per-saddle shares (blue) split between near 0 and near 1 with no relation to the gap, while the linear model (orange) predicts near 1 for most saddles.}\n    \\label{fig:saddles}\n\\end{figure}\n\nAt low noise the linear model of \\eqref{eq:linear} is close (TV 0.13), and curvature matters more than noise projection (noise-only TV 0.30).\nAt high noise, the model misses.\nOne third of saddles have an $\\etop$ share below 20\\%, though the model predicts a median of 0.98.\nPer-saddle outcomes stay reproducible anyway, with modal share 88\\%.\nSo exit there is near-deterministic along a non-$\\etop$ direction, which random linear growth cannot produce.\nPlausible causes are systematic noise-induced drift, or curvature that changes as thermal displacement moves the replica off the saddle.\nWe tested neither.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0143b6BMLjWtNC3SyJ5KnaSP","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgZszbToYwLGLfwqeMY2w","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01N3csst9AdfJvdR5mmW3m78","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/sgd_escape_time_distributions_20261030_140000_math01/paper_draft/sections/discussion.tex","content":"\\section{Discussion}\n\\label{sec:discussion}\n\n\\para{{\\bf Does the SDE picture hold?}}\nIn its scaling, largely yes: on \\fmnist, $\\tau\\eta$ depends on $\\etaB$ to within about 10\\%.\nIn its usual exponential detail, not cleanly.\nA short-time delay, many exit channels and strong differences between minima dominate at these noise levels, while E0 shows that the instrument recovers Kramers when the theory's conditions hold.\nSharpness, a common stand-in for escape difficulty, did not predict escape time here ($\\rho=0.08$ at $\\eta=0.1$ on \\fmnist).\n\n\\para{Alternative explanations.}\n{\\em $k>1$ as a measurement artefact.}\nThe converged quench and the Newton audit argue against it, and so does a robustness check: tripling quench length at 4 minima gives median ratios 1.00, 1.00, 1.10 and 0.64, and changes $k$ by at most 0.16.\nThe 0.64 is the sharpest minimum, where $\\tau=550$ spans only a few checks.\n{\\em Delay as in-well relaxation.}\nThis is consistent with the data, since excess loss relaxes over 1,000 or more steps, but we did not show it separately.\nE0 gives a smaller delay effect, with excess $k$ of at most 0.2, so network delays are larger, possibly because of slow soft modes.\n{\\em Adam's speed-up as calibration.}\nMatching excess loss is one reasonable choice, and the speed-up could partly reflect Adam's different stationary distribution.\nThe reversal at the default learning rate shows how much calibration matters.\n\n\\para{Relation to prior work.}\nOur temperature scaling agrees with \\citet{smith2018bayesian} and \\citet{jastrzebski2018three}.\nSteep dependence on $B$ agrees qualitatively with \\citet{xie2021diffusion} and \\citet{mori2022powerlaw}, though the exp-linear form fits worse in our range.\nThe claim of \\citet{zhou2020towards} that SGD escapes faster holds at Adam's default learning rate and reverses at matched loss, so the two views may differ mostly in calibration.\nThe finding of \\citet{xie2022adaptive} that momentum matters little for escaping sharp minima fits our \\sgdm ablation.\n\n\\para{Practical implications.}\nUse $\\etaB$ as the control variable and expect a steep response: batch size has far more leverage on trapping than a $\\sqrt{B}$ law suggests.\nDo not read Adam-versus-SGD escape claims without the learning-rate calibration.\nA single mean escape time also hides the delay and the spread between minima, so reports should give the distribution with censoring.\n\n\\para{Limitations.}\n\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=0pt,topsep=0pt]\n    \\item {\\em Scale.} We use tiny MLPs (115--610 parameters), small data and exact Hessians, and we show nothing for deep networks. Saddle results come from a different, smaller network than the minima results.\n    \\item {\\em Minima sample.} We use 12 + 6 minima. Narrow versus wide comparisons have $n=4$ per group and low power, and \\digitsdata has a small sharpness range.\n    \\item {\\em Censoring.} The 150,000-step cap hides the slowest behaviour. It removes 8 of 36 E1 conditions from H1, turns many large-$B$ medians into bounds, limits the Arrhenius test, and makes the \\digitsdata $\\etaB$ test inconclusive.\n    \\item {\\em Time resolution.} The check grid limits resolution below about 200 steps. Checks observe membership, so an exit and return between checks is missed.\n    \\item {\\em Definition.} Results describe first exit from a strict gradient-flow basin, and a coarser ``valley'' definition would give different times. Residual false exits are about 1.6\\%.\n    \\item {\\em Conditions.} An exact-replica start at $\\tstar$ builds in a transient. Two $\\eta=0.2$ runs at the sharpest minimum had $\\eta\\lmax>1$, where instability, not noise, may drive exit.\n    \\item {\\em Saddle sample.} Saddles come from descent trajectories with a low-index filter, mostly index 2, and they have weak curvature ($|\\lambda_1|$ from $2\\times10^{-4}$ to 0.03).\n\\end{itemize}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4804,"cache_read_input_tokens":115244,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4804},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f205d79e-9dcc-4336-8ac8-c0edce33ebd6","uuid":"27c291ab-abe2-4ac6-bffe-d26bebf4b9c5","timestamp":"2026-10-31T07:00:03.127Z","request_id":"req_011CgZszb7Tt386YqnLTY1kH","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01N3csst9AdfJvdR5mmW3m78":{"file_path":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/sgd_escape_time_distributions_20261030_140000_math01/paper_draft/sections/discussion.tex","content":"\\section{Discussion}\n\\label{sec:discussion}\n\n\\para{{\\bf Does the SDE picture hold?}}\nIn its scaling, largely yes: on \\fmnist, $\\tau\\eta$ depends on $\\etaB$ to within about 10\\%.\nIn its usual exponential detail, not cleanly.\nA short-time delay, many exit channels and strong differences between minima dominate at these noise levels, while E0 shows that the instrument recovers Kramers when the theory's conditions hold.\nSharpness, a common stand-in for escape difficulty, did not predict escape time here ($\\rho=0.08$ at $\\eta=0.1$ on \\fmnist).\n\n\\para{Alternative explanations.}\n{\\em $k>1$ as a measurement artefact.}\nThe converged quench and the Newton audit argue against it, and so does a robustness check: tripling quench length at 4 minima gives median ratios 1.00, 1.00, 1.10 and 0.64, and changes $k$ by at most 0.16.\nThe 0.64 is the sharpest minimum, where $\\tau=550$ spans only a few checks.\n{\\em Delay as in-well relaxation.}\nThis is consistent with the data, since excess loss relaxes over 1,000 or more steps, but we did not show it separately.\nE0 gives a smaller delay effect, with excess $k$ of at most 0.2, so network delays are larger, possibly because of slow soft modes.\n{\\em Adam's speed-up as calibration.}\nMatching excess loss is one reasonable choice, and the speed-up could partly reflect Adam's different stationary distribution.\nThe reversal at the default learning rate shows how much calibration matters.\n\n\\para{Relation to prior work.}\nOur temperature scaling agrees with \\citet{smith2018bayesian} and \\citet{jastrzebski2018three}.\nSteep dependence on $B$ agrees qualitatively with \\citet{xie2021diffusion} and \\citet{mori2022powerlaw}, though the exp-linear form fits worse in our range.\nThe claim of \\citet{zhou2020towards} that SGD escapes faster holds at Adam's default learning rate and reverses at matched loss, so the two views may differ mostly in calibration.\nThe finding of \\citet{xie2022adaptive} that momentum matters little for escaping sharp minima fits our \\sgdm ablation.\n\n\\para{Practical implications.}\nUse $\\etaB$ as the control variable and expect a steep response: batch size has far more leverage on trapping than a $\\sqrt{B}$ law suggests.\nDo not read Adam-versus-SGD escape claims without the learning-rate calibration.\nA single mean escape time also hides the delay and the spread between minima, so reports should give the distribution with censoring.\n\n\\para{Limitations.}\n\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=0pt,topsep=0pt]\n    \\item {\\em Scale.} We use tiny MLPs (115--610 parameters), small data and exact Hessians, and we show nothing for deep networks. Saddle results come from a different, smaller network than the minima results.\n    \\item {\\em Minima sample.} We use 12 + 6 minima. Narrow versus wide comparisons have $n=4$ per group and low power, and \\digitsdata has a small sharpness range.\n    \\item {\\em Censoring.} The 150,000-step cap hides the slowest behaviour. It removes 8 of 36 E1 conditions from H1, turns many large-$B$ medians into bounds, limits the Arrhenius test, and makes the \\digitsdata $\\etaB$ test inconclusive.\n    \\item {\\em Time resolution.} The check grid limits resolution below about 200 steps. Checks observe membership, so an exit and return between checks is missed.\n    \\item {\\em Definition.} Results describe first exit from a strict gradient-flow basin, and a coarser ``valley'' definition would give different times. Residual false exits are about 1.6\\%.\n    \\item {\\em Conditions.} An exact-replica start at $\\tstar$ builds in a transient. Two $\\eta=0.2$ runs at the sharpest minimum had $\\eta\\lmax>1$, where instability, not noise, may drive exit.\n    \\item {\\em Saddle sample.} Saddles come from descent trajectories with a low-index filter, mostly index 2, and they have weak curvature ($|\\lambda_1|$ from $2\\times10^{-4}$ to 0.03).\n\\end{itemize}\n"}}}
