onable choice, and the speed-up could partly reflect Adam's different stationary distribution.\nThe reversal at the default learning rate shows how much calibration matters.\n\n\\para{Relation to prior work.}\nOur temperature scaling agrees with \\citet{smith2018bayesian} and \\citet{jastrzebski2018three}.\nSteep dependence on $B$ agrees qualitatively with \\citet{xie2021diffusion} and \\citet{mori2022powerlaw}, though the exp-linear form fits worse in our range.\nThe claim of \\citet{zhou2020towards} that SGD escapes faster holds at Adam's default learning rate and reverses at matched loss, so the two views may differ mostly in calibration.\nThe finding of \\citet{xie2022adaptive} that momentum matters little for escaping sharp minima fits our \\sgdm ablation.\n\n\\para{Practical implications.}\nUse $\\etaB$ as the control variable and expect a steep response: batch size has far more leverage on trapping than a $\\sqrt{B}$ law suggests.\nDo not read Adam-versus-SGD escape claims without the learning-rate calibration.\nA single mean escape time also hides the delay and the spread between minima, so reports should give the distribution with censoring.\n\n\\para{Limitations.}\n\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=0pt,topsep=0pt]\n    \\item {\\em Scale.} We use tiny MLPs (115--610 parameters), small data and exact Hessians, and we show nothing for deep networks. Saddle results come from a different, smaller network than the minima results.\n    \\item {\\em Minima sample.} We use 12 + 6 minima. Narrow versus wide comparisons have $n=4$ per group and low power, and \\digitsdata has a small sharpness range.\n    \\item {\\em Censoring.} The 150,000-step cap hides the slowest behaviour. It removes 8 of 36 E1 conditions from H1, turns many large-$B$ medians into bounds, limits the Arrhenius test, and makes the \\digitsdata $\\etaB$ test inconclusive.\n    \\item {\\em Time resolution.} The check grid limits resolution below about 200 steps. Checks observe membership, so an exit and return between checks is missed.\n    \\item {\\em Definition.} Results describe first exit from a strict gradient-flow basin, and a coarser ``valley'' definition would give different times. Residual false exits are about 1.6\\%.\n    \\item {\\em Conditions.} An exact-replica start at $\\tstar$ builds in a transient. Two $\\eta=0.2$ runs at the sharpest minimum had $\\eta\\lmax>1$, where instability, not noise, may drive exit.\n    \\item {\\em Saddle sample.} Saddles come from descent trajectories with a low-index filter, mostly index 2, and they have weak curvature ($|\\lambda_1|$ from $2\\times10^{-4}$ to 0.03).\n\\end{itemize}\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01N3csst9AdfJvdR5mmW3m78","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgZszbToYwLGLfwqeMY2w","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015d8rN22FhdwtKD8DizcMjE","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/sgd_escape_time_distributions_20261030_140000_math01/paper_draft/sections/conclusion.tex","content":"\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n\nWe measure escape-time distributions of SGD and Adam from exact minima and saddles of small tanh networks, and we test four numerical claims against criteria fixed in advance.\nEscape from these basins is a discrete hop with a non-constant hazard, shaped mainly by a delay within a minimum and by large differences between minima, not by a Weibull law with $k$ in 0.7--1.3 tied to geometry.\n$\\etaB$ is the right control variable on the main landscape, but $\\tau$ scales about as $B^{2}$, not $\\sqrt{B}$.\nAdam's advantage is real at matched in-basin loss (about $2\\times$, from adaptive scaling and not momentum), is not shown to be specific to narrow basins, and disappears at the default learning rate.\nSaddle exit follows $\\etop$ at low noise, the gap does not predict how often, and at high noise exit is reproducible along other directions.\n\nThe main takeaway is that no single shape parameter summarizes escape: delay, minimum identity and noise level each change the answer.\nFuture work should extend run lengths at large $B$ to settle power law versus Arrhenius, start from conditioned quasi-stationary states to measure the delay directly, test the non-$\\etop$ saddle exits for noise-induced drift, and repeat these measurements on wider or deeper networks with Hessian-vector quenching.\nOne open question remains: why does the hazard fall late within a single minimum, through competing channels or slow drift to harder regions?\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4804,"cache_read_input_tokens":115244,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4804},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f205d79e-9dcc-4336-8ac8-c0edce33ebd6","uuid":"6777f527-96e6-4102-be7f-046392795eeb","timestamp":"2026-10-31T07:00:08.377Z","request_id":"req_011CgZszb7Tt386YqnLTY1kH","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_015d8rN22FhdwtKD8DizcMjE":{"file_path":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/sgd_escape_time_distributions_20261030_140000_math01/paper_draft/sections/conclusion.tex","content":"\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n\nWe measure escape-time distributions of SGD and Adam from exact minima and saddles of small tanh networks, and we test four numerical claims against criteria fixed in advance.\nEscape from these basins is a discrete hop with a non-constant hazard, shaped mainly by a delay within a minimum and by large differences between minima, not by a Weibull law with $k$ in 0.7--1.3 tied to geometry.\n$\\etaB$ is the right control variable on the main landscape, but $\\tau$ scales about as $B^{2}$, not $\\sqrt{B}$.\nAdam's advantage is real at matched in-basin loss (about $2\\times$, from adaptive scaling and not momentum), is not shown to be specific to narrow basins, and disappears at the default learning rate.\nSaddle exit follows $\\etop$ at low noise, the gap does not predict how often, and at high noise exit is reproducible along other directions.\n\nThe main takeaway is that no single shape parameter summarizes escape: delay, minimum identity and noise level each change the answer.\nFuture work should extend run lengths at large $B$ to settle power law versus Arrhenius, start from conditioned quasi-stationary states to measure the delay directly, test the non-$\\etop$ saddle exits for noise-induced drift, and repeat these measurements on wider or deeper networks with Hessian-vector quenching.\nOne open question remains: why does the hazard fall late within a single minimum, through competing channels or slow drift to harder regions?\n"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015d8rN22FhdwtKD8DizcMjE","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/sgd_escape_time_distributions_20261030_140000_math01/paper_draft/sections/conclusion.tex (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f205d79e-9dcc-4336-8ac8-c0edce33ebd6","uuid":"9be81e77-cdd5-46c3-8b45-e7265c1c98bd","timestamp":"2026-10-31T07:00:08.414Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/sgd_escape_time_distributions_20261030_140000_math01/paper_draft/sections/conclusion.tex","content":"\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n\nWe measure escape-time distributions of SGD and Adam from exact minima and saddles of small tanh networks, and we test four numerical claims against criteria fixed in advance.\nEscape from these basins is a discrete hop with a non-constant hazard, shaped mainly by a delay within a minimum and by large differences between minima, not by a Weibull law with $k$ in 0.7--1.3 tied to geometry.\n$\\etaB$ is the right control variable on the main landscape, but $\\tau$ scales about as $B^{2}$, not $\\sqrt{B}$.\nAdam's advantage is real at matched in-basin loss (about $2\\times$, from adaptive scaling and not momentum), is not shown to be specific to narrow basins, and disappears at the default learning rate.\nSaddle exit follows $\\etop$ at low noise, the gap does not predict how often, and at high noise exit is reproducible along other directions.\n\nThe main takeaway is that no single shape parameter summarizes escape: delay, minimum identity and noise level each change the answer.\nFuture work should extend run lengths at large $B$ to settle power law versus Arrhenius, start from conditioned quasi-stationary states to measure the delay directly, test the non-$\\etop$ saddle exits for noise-induced drift, and repeat these measurements on wider or deeper networks with Hessian-vector quenching.\nOne open question remains: why does the hazard fall late within a single minimum, through competing channels or slow drift to harder regions?\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_015d8rN22FhdwtKD8DizcMjE","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgZszbToYwLGLfwqeMY2w","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015V5dBrErEMa2YfWDHfbZ5k","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/sgd_escape_time_distributions_20261030_140000_math01/paper_draft/appendix/preregistration.tex","content":"\\section{Preregistered Criteria and Deviations}\n\\label{app:prereg}\n\n\\Tabref{tab:prereg} lists the criteria we fixed before any experiment, with the outcome of each.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n    \\centering\n    \\caption{Preregistered criteria and outcomes. Values are for \\fmnist unless noted.}\n    \\label{tab:prereg}\n    \\resizebox{\\textwidth}{!}{%\n    \\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}\n        \\toprule\n        \\textbf{ID} & \\textbf{Criterion} & \\textbf{Outcome} \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        H1a & LRT rejects $k=1$ (FDR 5\\%) in a majority, $|\\Delta\\text{AIC}|>10$ & Met: 86\\% and 75\\% \\\\\n        H1b & $k$ in [0.7, 1.3] in $\\ge$90\\%, with both $k<1$ and $k>1$ present & Not met: 32\\% \\\\\n        H1c & Spearman $\\rho(k,\\log\\text{sharpness})$ significant, $p<.05$ & Not met: $p=.77$ \\\\\n        H2a & Narrow $\\tau_{\\text{SGD}}/\\tau_{\\text{Adam}}$ CI inside [2, 5] & Not met: [1.89, 5.18] \\\\\n        H2b & Wide ratio CI inside [0.67, 1.5] & Not met: [1.24, 3.22] \\\\\n        H2c & Narrow/wide ratio-of-ratios $>1$ & Not significant: 1.46 [0.74, 3.09] \\\\\n        H3a & Slope CI contains 0.5, excludes 0 and 1 & Not met: [1.64, 2.41] \\\\\n        H3b & Power law beats Arrhenius by AIC & Met in the observed range: $-$16.7 vs $+$16.1 \\\\\n        H3c & Same $\\etaB$ gives same $\\tau\\eta$ within CI & Partly met: 1.03 [0.97, 1.11] and 0.89 [0.86, 0.93] (5 minima) \\\\\n        H4a & Top-eigenvector accuracy CI overlaps [70, 85]\\% and exceeds chance & Met at lower noise ($r=1$); not at high noise \\\\\n        H4b & Accuracy increases with gap & Not met: $\\rho$ from $-0.07$ to $+0.23$, n.s. \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n    }\n\\end{table}\n\n\\para{Deviations from the plan.}\n\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=0pt,topsep=0pt]\n    \\item We replaced the planned short quench with the converged quench, before production, for the reason in \\secref{sec:method}.\n    \\item We added the barrier-or-loss rule after the Newton audit and reran the 10 affected runs with the same seeds.\n    \\item We moved the base condition from $\\eta=0.2$ to $\\eta=0.1$, because at $\\eta=0.2$ escape competes with in-well relaxation. We kept $\\eta=0.2$ as the hot condition.\n    \\item We made \\fmnist at weight decay $10^{-4}$ the main landscape, because weight decay made \\digitsdata minima nearly uniform in sharpness.\n    \\item We used a smaller network for saddles, because mode-following Newton did not converge at 290 parameters. We rejected symmetric saddles, because per-sample gradients vanish along their escape directions.\n    \\item We saved but did not analyse the secondary loss-level and radius first-passage definitions, and we analysed quench-length and exit-rule robustness instead.\n    \\item 165 of 166 jobs finished. The missing job is default-learning-rate Adam at the sharpest minimum.\n    \\item We did not fix the H4 radius in advance, so we report all three radii and all three noise levels.\n\\end{itemize}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4804,"cache_read_input_tokens":115244,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4804},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f205d79e-9dcc-4336-8ac8-c0edce33ebd6","uuid":"321be004-10b2-4df9-acb9-c611ecf2d2aa","timestamp":"2026-10-31T07:00:19.377Z","request_id":"req_011CgZszb7Tt386YqnLTY1kH","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_015V5dBrErEMa2YfWDHfbZ5k":{"file_path":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/sgd_escape_time_distributions_20261030_140000_math01/paper_draft/appendix/preregistration.tex","content":"\\section{Preregistered Criteria and Deviations}\n\\label{app:prereg}\n\n\\Tabref{tab:prereg} lists the criteria we fixed before any experiment, with the outcome of each.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n    \\centering\n    \\caption{Preregistered criteria and outcomes. Values are for \\fmnist unless noted.}\n    \\label{tab:prereg}\n    \\resizebox{\\textwidth}{!}{%\n    \\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}\n        \\toprule\n        \\textbf{ID} & \\textbf{Criterion} & \\textbf{Outcome} \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        H1a & LRT rejects $k=1$ (FDR 5\\%) in a majority, $|\\Delta\\text{AIC}|>10$ & Met: 86\\% and 75\\% \\\\\n        H1b & $k$ in [0.7, 1.3] in $\\ge$90\\%, with both $k<1$ and $k>1$ present & Not met: 32\\% \\\\\n        H1c & Spearman $\\rho(k,\\log\\text{sharpness})$ significant, $p<.05$ & Not met: $p=.77$ \\\\\n        H2a & Narrow $\\tau_{\\text{SGD}}/\\tau_{\\text{Adam}}$ CI inside [2, 5] & Not met: [1.89, 5.18] \\\\\n        H2b & Wide ratio CI inside [0.67, 1.5] & Not met: [1.24, 3.22] \\\\\n        H2c & Narrow/wide ratio-of-ratios $>1$ & Not significant: 1.46 [0.74, 3.09] \\\\\n        H3a & Slope CI contains 0.5, excludes 0 and 1 & Not met: [1.64, 2.41] \\\\\n        H3b & Power law beats Arrhenius by AIC & Met in the observed range: $-$16.7 vs $+$16.1 \\\\\n        H3c & Same $\\etaB$ gives same $\\tau\\eta$ within CI & Partly met: 1.03 [0.97, 1.11] and 0.89 [0.86, 0.93] (5 minima) \\\\\n        H4a & Top-eigenvector accuracy CI overlaps [70, 85]\\% and exceeds chance & Met at lower noise ($r=1$); not at high noise \\\\\n        H4b & Accuracy increases with gap & Not met: $\\rho$ from $-0.07$ to $+0.23$, n.s. \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n    }\n\\end{table}\n\n\\para{Deviations from the plan.}\n\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=0pt,topsep=0pt]\n    \\item We replaced the planned short quench with the converged quench, before production, for the reason in \\secref{sec:method}.\n    \\item We added the barrier-or-loss rule after the Newton audit and reran the 10 affected runs with the same seeds.\n    \\item We moved the base condition from $\\eta=0.2$ to $\\eta=0.1$, because at $\\eta=0.2$ escape competes with in-well relaxation. We kept $\\eta=0.2$ as the hot condition.\n    \\item We made \\fmnist at weight decay $10^{-4}$ the main landscape, because weight decay made \\digitsdata minima nearly uniform in sharpness.\n    \\item We used a smaller network for saddles, because mode-following Newton did not converge at 290 parameters. We rejected symmetric saddles, because per-sample gradients vanish along their escape directions.\n    \\item We saved but did not analyse the secondary loss-level and radius first-passage definitions, and we analysed quench-length and exit-rule robustness instead.\n    \\item 165 of 166 jobs finished. The missing job is default-learning-rate Adam at the sharpest minimum.\n    \\item We did not fix the H4 radius in advance, so we report all three radii and all three noise levels.\n\\end{itemize}\n"}}}
