{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PiXKyTYJygaBdQzfmVpMgR","type":"tool_result","content":"       Escape Times from Neural Network Basins:\n    Delay and Heterogeneity, Not a Weibull Shape Law\n\n\n                                              NeuriCo\n\n\n\n                                             Abstract\n         Theories of flat-minima bias rest on mean escape-time formulas, yet the full distri-\n         bution of how long an optimizer stays in a basin is rarely measured. We measure\n         it: we run SGD and Adam from exact minima and true saddles of small tanh\n         networks on FASHION -MNIST and D IGITS, across 165 conditions and 17,474\n         exits, and decide exit by converged quenching. Our simulator recovers the exact\n         Kramers rate on a double well (median ratio 1.07). We test four numerical claims,\n         and none holds as stated. Exponential times are rejected in 86% of conditions,\n         but Weibull is the best model in only 4 of 28, its shape k lies in 0.7–1.3 in only\n         32%, and k is unrelated to curvature (ρ = −0.06). Most of the deviation from\n         k = 1 comes from a start-up delay (refitting after early exits gives k = 1.03)\n         and from mixing across minima (pooling gives    √ k = 0.52–0.74). Doubling the\n         batch size multiplies escape time by 5.1, not 2, while η/B still sets the time\n         scale on FASHION -MNIST to within about 10%. Adam escapes about 2× faster\n         than SGD at matched in-basin loss, with no demonstrated dependence on basin\n         width, but makes no exits at its default learning rate. Saddle exit follows the top\n         eigenvector 82–86% of the time at low noise and 54% at high noise, and the eigen-\n         value gap does not predict that share. Escape is a discrete, delayed hop whose rate\n         depends on noise and on the specific minimum.\n\n\n1    Introduction\na-04.png\n113","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f205d79e-9dcc-4336-8ac8-c0edce33ebd6","uuid":"4d750485-eab0-4992-b9a9-b886c3569c5e","timestamp":"2026-10-31T07:01:29.944Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"       Escape Times from Neural Network Basins:\n    Delay and Heterogeneity, Not a Weibull Shape Law\n\n\n                                              NeuriCo\n\n\n\n                                             Abstract\n         Theories of flat-minima bias rest on mean escape-time formulas, yet the full distri-\n         bution of how long an optimizer stays in a basin is rarely measured. We measure\n         it: we run SGD and Adam from exact minima and true saddles of small tanh\n         networks on FASHION -MNIST and D IGITS, across 165 conditions and 17,474\n         exits, and decide exit by converged quenching. Our simulator recovers the exact\n         Kramers rate on a double well (median ratio 1.07). We test four numerical claims,\n         and none holds as stated. Exponential times are rejected in 86% of conditions,\n         but Weibull is the best model in only 4 of 28, its shape k lies in 0.7–1.3 in only\n         32%, and k is unrelated to curvature (ρ = −0.06). Most of the deviation from\n         k = 1 comes from a start-up delay (refitting after early exits gives k = 1.03)\n         and from mixing across minima (pooling gives    √ k = 0.52–0.74). Doubling the\n         batch size multiplies escape time by 5.1, not 2, while η/B still sets the time\n         scale on FASHION -MNIST to within about 10%. Adam escapes about 2× faster\n         than SGD at matched in-basin loss, with no demonstrated dependence on basin\n         width, but makes no exits at its default learning rate. Saddle exit follows the top\n         eigenvector 82–86% of the time at low noise and 54% at high noise, and the eigen-\n         value gap does not predict that share. Escape is a discrete, delayed hop whose rate\n         depends on noise and on the specific minimum.\n\n\n1    Introduction\na-04.png\n113","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01PiXKyTYJygaBdQzfmVpMgR","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
